From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: realign a subtitle file on the video's own
 subtitles

A subtitle file made for another release of a film is off: another
intro makes every line late or early by the same amount, and a PAL
release, which runs 4% fast, makes them drift further out as the film
goes on. The viewer sees the lines come before or after the voices and
can only nudge the subtitle delay by hand, which a drift defeats.

The video usually carries the answer: its own subtitle tracks, in
whatever language, start their lines where speech or a sign starts. So
when a SubRip file beside the video opens, half a minute of the video is
read at six points across it, in the background, and the starts of one
of its subtitle tracks - a text one, else a Blu-ray's picture one, never
a forced one - are laid against the file's. Every pair of starts votes
for the offset that would make them one line's, under each frame-rate
ratio a release can be off by (25 or 24 against 23.976, and back); the
best few are refined on the lines they match.

One change then moves every line alike, so a line that translates
something written on screen, which no speech marks, keeps its place
among the others. It is made only when it lines up at least eight of
the reference's lines and two in five of them, when no other change
comes close, when the times as they are do not already fit, and when it
holds in every stretch read: a subtitle that is right is never touched,
and one cut differently from the video - a scene more, after which no
single change fits - is left alone. Over 300 made-up films it moved no
right subtitle, accepted no other film's track, and found 296 of 300
moved ones exactly; of 300 files with a minute added after the first
stretch, it moved one, whose only line there was the one it missed.

The video is the one the player opened, given with the subtitle's hints.
It is read without the file cache, in the same way playback reads it, so
that both count time from the same start; closing the subtitle aborts a
read stalled on the network. Discs, read through their navigation, are
not sampled. What was found is kept for the session.

The subtitle shows as it is meanwhile. When a change is found, what had
reached the screen is cleared and shown again moved, and what is still
to come moves as it comes: nothing is translated or corrected twice. It
is logged.

Player > Subtitles > Repair gains "Realign with the video", on.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
